# F1. The `at_barrier` guard, without which `OnBarrier` fires on every drive.
#
# The failure `ring_flush` exists to make visible is a policy that quietly
# starts firing when it should not. Twenty-four passing tests is also an
# accurate description of twenty-four tests that could never fail; the only way
# to tell those apart is to commit the violation.
#
# The round predicted two failures and got four. The extras show what the real
# detector is, and it is not the test named after the job. Every one of them
# contains an assertion that some drive returns `NotTriggered`. That shape
# catches this, and it turns up incidentally in tests written for other reasons.
#
# The count below is 6 rather than the round's own 4. The discrepancy is not
# erosion in either direction. The round's number was wrong. Its output reads
# "16/23 tests run", which is nextest stopping early on failures by default, so
# 4 was however many had run when it gave up rather than how many catch this.
# Re-measured 2026-08-29 with `--no-fail-fast`: **6**. The round's conclusion
# survives its arithmetic. The detector really is the not-firing assertion, and
# two more tests hold this line than the round knew. G12 runs `--no-fail-fast`
# for exactly this reason.
#
# It also corrected the crate's own module documentation, which had claimed this
# was the failure "no positive test can see". The distinction that matters is
# between assertions about firing and assertions about *not* firing, not between
# positive and negative tests. A suite made entirely of "after
# `drive_at_barrier`, `Flushed`" would miss it; four of ours do not, by
# accident.

id: F1
crate: ring_flush
file: src/lib.rs
round: 2026-08-28
round-failures: 6
detector: on_barrier_never_fires_without_an_announcement

--- from
      FlushPolicy::OnBarrier => {
        if at_barrier {
--- to
      FlushPolicy::OnBarrier => {
        if true {  // PROBE ONLY — G12, was `at_barrier`
--- end
